INSERT INTO `as_meditations` (`id`, `element`, `title`, `slug`, `description`, `content_json`, `duration_minutes`, `sort_order`, `created_at`) VALUES (1,'Wood','Spring Renewal','spring-renewal','A guided meditation for Wood element energy. Release frustration, cultivate flexibility, and nurture growth through liver and gallbladder meridian awareness.','{\"intro\":\"Find a comfortable seated position and gently close your eyes. Take three deep breaths, allowing your body to settle. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Wood element governs the liver and gallbladder, and is associated with spring, a time of new growth, vision, and the free flow of energy. This meditation will help you release stagnant anger or frustration and cultivate the positive Wood qualities of kindness, flexibility, and creative vision.\",\"steps\":[{\"title\":\"Settling In\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"Bring your awareness to your breath. Feel the natural rhythm of inhaling and exhaling. With each exhale, let go of any tension in your shoulders, jaw, and hands. Imagine yourself sitting beneath a great tree, its branches swaying gently in a spring breeze.\"},{\"title\":\"Liver Meridian Awareness\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"Bring your attention to the right side of your body, just beneath your ribcage where the liver resides. Breathe warmth and green healing light into this area. The liver in TCM is the organ of planning and vision. As you breathe here, notice any tightness or holding, this is often where we store frustration and unexpressed anger.\"},{\"title\":\"Releasing Frustration\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"With each exhale, imagine a gentle green mist carrying away any frustration, irritation, or resentment you have been holding. You do not need to analyze these feelings, simply let them dissolve with each breath out. Like a tree shedding old leaves, you are making space for fresh growth.\"},{\"title\":\"Cultivating Flexibility\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Now imagine your spine as the trunk of a young tree, strong yet flexible. With each breath, feel yourself becoming more supple, more adaptable. The healthy Wood element bends without breaking. Repeat silently: I flow with life. I am flexible and strong.\"},{\"title\":\"Vision and Growth\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Bring to mind something you wish to grow or create in your life. See it as a small green sprout emerging from rich soil. With each breath, send it nourishing energy. The Wood element gives us the power to envision our future and move toward it with purpose.\"},{\"title\":\"Kindness Integration\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"The positive emotion of balanced Wood is kindness and generosity. Let a feeling of warmth and goodwill fill your chest. Extend this kindness first to yourself, then outward to those around you. Feel the expansive, generous quality of healthy Wood energy.\"}],\"closing\":\"Gently bring your awareness back to the room. Wiggle your fingers and toes. When you are ready, open your eyes. Carry the qualities of spring, flexibility, vision, and kindness, with you into the rest of your day. Remember: like the tree, your strength comes from your ability to bend and grow.\",\"tcm_connection\":\"The Wood element in TCM governs the Liver and Gallbladder meridians. When balanced, Wood energy manifests as clear vision, good planning ability, and emotional flexibility. When imbalanced, it can show as anger, frustration, headaches, eye problems, or muscle tension. This meditation supports liver qi flow and emotional release. Practice it especially during spring or when you feel stuck or irritable. Complement with sour foods like lemon water and green leafy vegetables. Incorporate gentle stretching exercises.\"}',12,1,'2026-02-25 13:51:03');
INSERT INTO `as_meditations` (`id`, `element`, `title`, `slug`, `description`, `content_json`, `duration_minutes`, `sort_order`, `created_at`) VALUES (2,'Fire','Heart\'s Flame','hearts-flame','A guided meditation for Fire element energy. Cultivate compassion, calm overexcitement, and nourish your heart through mindful warmth and connection.','{\"intro\":\"Settle into a comfortable position and softly close your eyes. Take three slow, deep breaths. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Fire element governs the heart and small intestine, and is linked to summer, a time of warmth, joy, and connection. This meditation will help you balance your inner fire: calming anxiety or overstimulation while cultivating the positive Fire qualities of joy, compassion, and meaningful connection.\",\"steps\":[{\"title\":\"Centering the Heart\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"Place one hand gently over your heart center. Feel the steady rhythm of your heartbeat beneath your palm. This is the seat of your Fire element, the emperor of all organs in TCM. With each beat, acknowledge the faithful work of your heart, both physically and emotionally.\"},{\"title\":\"Warming Breath\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Breathe in through your nose, imagining warm, golden-red light entering your chest. Hold for a moment, feeling this warmth expand. Exhale slowly through your mouth. This warmth is not the wild fire of anxiety, but the steady, nourishing flame of a hearth fire, safe, warming, and constant.\"},{\"title\":\"Calming the Spirit\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"In TCM, the heart houses the Shen, your spirit and consciousness. When Fire is excessive, the Shen becomes restless: anxiety, insomnia, scattered thoughts. Imagine a still lake reflecting a sunset. Each ripple of anxious thought settles as you breathe. The surface becomes calm and mirror-like. Your Shen is settling.\"},{\"title\":\"Cultivating Joy\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"The true emotion of balanced Fire is joy, not manic excitement, but a deep, quiet contentment. Bring to mind something that fills you with genuine warmth: a loved one, a beautiful memory, a moment of pure connection. Let this joy radiate from your heart center outward through your whole body.\"},{\"title\":\"Compassion Practice\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Extend the warmth of your heart outward. First to someone you love, may they be happy and well. Then to someone neutral, may they know peace. Then to someone difficult, may they find healing. Finally, to all beings, may all hearts know the warmth of genuine connection. This is Fire in its highest expression.\"},{\"title\":\"Integration\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"Bring both hands to rest on your thighs. Feel the balanced warmth throughout your body, neither too hot nor too cold. Your inner fire is steady, your spirit calm, your heart open. Rest in this balanced state for a few more breaths.\"}],\"closing\":\"Slowly return your awareness to the room. Notice the sounds around you. Gently open your eyes. The balanced Fire element gives us the ability to connect meaningfully with others while maintaining our inner peace. Carry this warmth, this quiet joy, into your interactions today.\",\"tcm_connection\":\"The Fire element governs the Heart and Small Intestine meridians, as well as the Pericardium and Triple Burner. The heart houses the Shen (spirit/mind), making it central to emotional wellbeing, sleep quality, and mental clarity. When balanced, Fire manifests as appropriate joy, warm social connection, and clear communication. When imbalanced, it can show as anxiety, insomnia, excessive talking, heart palpitations, or social withdrawal. This meditation supports heart qi and calms the Shen. Practice during summer or when feeling anxious. Complement with bitter foods like dark chocolate, green tea, and arugula, which help clear excess heat.\"}',10,2,'2026-02-25 13:51:03');
INSERT INTO `as_meditations` (`id`, `element`, `title`, `slug`, `description`, `content_json`, `duration_minutes`, `sort_order`, `created_at`) VALUES (3,'Earth','Grounded Center','grounded-center','A guided meditation for Earth element energy. Release worry, find stability, and strengthen your digestive center through grounding awareness and nourishment.','{\"intro\":\"Find your comfortable position and close your eyes. Take three full breaths, feeling your connection to the ground beneath you. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Earth element governs the spleen and stomach, and is linked to late summer and transitions, times of harvest, nourishment, and centering. This meditation will help you release excessive worry and overthinking while cultivating the positive Earth qualities of stability, trust, and groundedness.\",\"steps\":[{\"title\":\"Grounding\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Feel the weight of your body pressing down. Whether sitting or lying, notice every point of contact with the surface beneath you. Imagine roots growing from the base of your spine, extending deep into the earth. These roots anchor you, stable and secure. You are held. You are supported.\"},{\"title\":\"Belly Awareness\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Bring your attention to your center, your belly, the home of the spleen and stomach in TCM. Place your hands gently on your abdomen. Breathe into this area, feeling it expand and soften. This is your body\'s center of digestion, not just of food, but of thoughts, emotions, and life experiences.\"},{\"title\":\"Releasing Worry\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"The Earth element, when out of balance, generates excessive worry and circular thinking. Notice any thoughts that spin without resolution. Instead of engaging with them, imagine placing each worry into a warm bowl of golden light at your center. The Earth\'s gentle warmth transforms these worries into compost, they become nourishment for growth rather than a burden.\"},{\"title\":\"Nourishment\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Imagine a warm, golden light filling your belly, the color of ripe grain, of honey, of late afternoon sun. This is the nourishing energy of balanced Earth. With each breath, this golden warmth strengthens your digestion, your center, your ability to receive and give nourishment. You are enough. You have enough.\"},{\"title\":\"Trust and Stability\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"The positive emotion of balanced Earth is trust and faithfulness. Feel a deep sense of stability in your core. Repeat silently: I trust the ground beneath me. I trust my body\'s wisdom. I am centered and stable. Like the earth itself, you are reliable, nurturing, and steady.\"},{\"title\":\"Returning Home\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"Take a final deep breath into your belly. Feel the solidity and warmth of your center. No matter what swirls around you, this center is always here, your home base, your ground. You can return to this feeling of stability anytime by simply breathing into your belly and feeling your feet on the ground.\"}],\"closing\":\"Slowly open your eyes. Feel the ground beneath you. Notice how centered and stable you feel. The Earth element reminds us that we are always supported, always nourished, always home. Carry this grounded feeling with you, especially during transitions or times of change.\",\"tcm_connection\":\"The Earth element governs the Spleen and Stomach meridians, the foundation of post-natal qi (energy). A strong Earth element means good digestion, clear thinking, and emotional stability. When imbalanced, Earth manifests as excessive worry, digestive issues, fatigue, loose stools, or difficulty concentrating. The Spleen is also responsible for transforming and transporting nutrients, so its health affects energy levels throughout the body. Practice this meditation during seasonal transitions or when overthinking. Complement with warm, cooked foods, root vegetables, sweet potatoes, and ginger tea. Maintain regular meal times.\"}',11,3,'2026-02-25 13:51:03');
INSERT INTO `as_meditations` (`id`, `element`, `title`, `slug`, `description`, `content_json`, `duration_minutes`, `sort_order`, `created_at`) VALUES (4,'Metal','Autumn Release','autumn-release','A guided meditation for Metal element energy. Process grief, find clarity, and support your lungs through the art of letting go and finding what is essential.','{\"intro\":\"Come to stillness and close your eyes. Take three cleansing breaths, breathing in through the nose and out through the mouth with an audible sigh. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Metal element governs the lungs and large intestine, and is linked to autumn, a time of letting go, refinement, and finding what is essential. This meditation will help you process grief and sadness while cultivating the positive Metal qualities of courage, clarity, and inner worth.\",\"steps\":[{\"title\":\"Breath Awareness\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"The lungs are the primary organ of the Metal element. Bring all your attention to your breath. Feel the air entering through your nostrils, cool and fresh. Feel it filling your lungs, expanding your ribcage. Feel the warm exhale leaving your body. The breath is a constant cycle of receiving and releasing. This is the essence of Metal: knowing what to take in and what to let go.\"},{\"title\":\"Lung Expansion\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Take a deeper breath than usual, filling your lungs completely. Hold for three counts. Then exhale fully, gently squeezing out every last bit of air. Repeat this three times. With each cycle, imagine your lungs becoming clearer, brighter, more spacious. Clean, white light fills your chest with each inhale.\"},{\"title\":\"Acknowledging Grief\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"The emotion of Metal is grief. This is not weakness, it is the natural response to loss and change. Allow yourself to acknowledge any losses you carry: people, places, phases of life, versions of yourself. You do not need to fix or resolve anything. Simply acknowledge: I have lost, and that matters. Hold these losses with tenderness, as you would hold a fallen autumn leaf.\"},{\"title\":\"The Art of Letting Go\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"Now, with each exhale, practice letting go. Not forcing or pushing away, but gently releasing, the way a tree releases its leaves in autumn. They fall naturally when the time is right. What are you ready to release? Old patterns, outdated beliefs, relationships that no longer serve? With each breath out, let one thing fall away naturally.\"},{\"title\":\"Finding the Essential\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Metal is about refinement, finding the precious ore within the mountain of experience. As you let go of what is not essential, notice what remains. What is truly valuable in your life? What do you hold sacred? Let these things shine with a clear, bright light. This is your inner gold.\"},{\"title\":\"Courage and Worth\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"The positive expression of balanced Metal is courage and a deep sense of self-worth that does not depend on external validation. Feel this within you now: a quiet, dignified strength. Repeat silently: I am worthy as I am. I have the courage to release what no longer serves me. I honor what is essential.\"}],\"closing\":\"Take one final deep, cleansing breath. Feel the clarity in your chest and mind. Open your eyes. The Metal element teaches us that true richness comes from knowing what is essential and having the courage to release the rest. Like autumn, letting go creates space for the new.\",\"tcm_connection\":\"The Metal element governs the Lung and Large Intestine meridians. The lungs manage the body\'s protective qi (Wei Qi), acting as the first line of immune defense. The large intestine handles elimination of waste. Together, they govern the boundary between self and world, what we take in and what we let go. When Metal is balanced, we have clear boundaries, strong immunity, and emotional resilience. When imbalanced, we may experience chronic grief, respiratory issues, skin problems, constipation, or difficulty letting go. Practice this meditation in autumn or during times of loss. Complement with pungent foods like ginger, garlic, and onion, and pears for lung moisture. Incorporate deep breathing exercises.\"}',10,4,'2026-02-25 13:51:03');
INSERT INTO `as_meditations` (`id`, `element`, `title`, `slug`, `description`, `content_json`, `duration_minutes`, `sort_order`, `created_at`) VALUES (5,'Water','Deep Stillness','deep-stillness','A guided meditation for Water element energy. Transform fear into wisdom, build courage, and nourish your kidneys through deep stillness and inner listening.','{\"intro\":\"Find your most comfortable resting position, this meditation works beautifully lying down. Close your eyes and take three slow, deep breaths. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, the Water element governs the kidneys and bladder, and is linked to winter, a time of rest, conservation, and deep inner work. This meditation will help you transform fear and insecurity into wisdom and willpower, the positive qualities of balanced Water.\",\"steps\":[{\"title\":\"Descending Into Stillness\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"With each exhale, allow yourself to sink deeper into rest. Imagine yourself descending gently, like a stone settling to the bottom of a deep, clear pool. There is no effort required. Gravity and breath carry you down into stillness. The deeper you go, the quieter it becomes.\"},{\"title\":\"Kidney Warming\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"Bring your awareness to your lower back, where the kidneys reside on either side of the spine. These are the root of all your body\'s energy in TCM, the storehouse of your constitutional essence (Jing). Imagine a deep, warm blue light gently warming your kidneys. With each breath, this warmth grows, nourishing your deepest reserves of energy.\"},{\"title\":\"Facing Fear\",\"duration_seconds\":120,\"instruction\":\"The emotion of Water is fear, not just the dramatic kind, but also the subtle background anxiety of insecurity, uncertainty, and inadequacy. Without judgment, notice where fear lives in your body. Perhaps in your belly, your chest, or your throat. Simply observe it. In TCM, fear is not an enemy but a signal, it shows us where we need to build more trust in our foundation.\"},{\"title\":\"Building the Deep Reserve\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"Imagine your kidneys as ancient wells of deep, pure water. With each breath, these wells fill a little more. This is your reserve of vital energy, wisdom, and willpower. You do not need to use this energy now, simply let it accumulate. In winter, nature does not grow outward; it conserves and stores. Allow yourself this same permission to simply be, without doing.\"},{\"title\":\"Wisdom Rising\",\"duration_seconds\":90,\"instruction\":\"The positive expression of balanced Water is wisdom. Not intellectual knowledge, but the deep knowing that comes from stillness and experience. In this quiet state, notice any insights that arise naturally. Do not chase them, let them surface like bubbles from a deep spring. Your body holds wisdom that your thinking mind cannot access. Listen.\"},{\"title\":\"Willpower and Courage\",\"duration_seconds\":60,\"instruction\":\"Feel the deep reserve of energy in your lower back and belly. This is the source of genuine willpower, not the force of will, but the persistent, gentle power of water that can carve through stone over time. Repeat silently: I am deeply resourced. I have the wisdom and courage to navigate any challenge. My foundation is strong.\"}],\"closing\":\"Rest for a moment longer in this deep stillness. Then slowly, gently, bring movement back to your fingers and toes. Stretch if you wish. When you are ready, open your eyes. The Water element reminds us that our greatest strength comes from rest, our deepest wisdom from silence, and our truest courage from knowing our own depths.\",\"tcm_connection\":\"The Water element governs the Kidney and Bladder meridians. The kidneys store Jing (essence), our constitutional vitality that determines longevity, fertility, bone health, and brain function. They are considered the root of all yin and yang in the body. When Water is balanced, we feel courageous, wise, and deeply resourced. When imbalanced, we experience fear, low back pain, knee weakness, premature aging, poor memory, or frequent urination. The kidneys are especially vulnerable to overwork, lack of sleep, and chronic stress. Practice this meditation in winter or when feeling depleted. Complement with black foods (black beans, black sesame, seaweed), bone broth, walnuts, and importantly, adequate sleep and rest.\"}',13,5,'2026-02-25 13:51:03');
